I want to look at the state of adult education in Ireland. I am suggesting that the 
state here means both the condition in which we now find adult education and 
the role of the Irish State in adult education. I will briefly outline 
• some recent developments in adult education; 
• make some critical comments on the state of adult education; 
• look at the links between the State and the economy and civil society that have important implications for adult education.

Recognition of prior learning (RPL) have started well at the Finnish universities of applied sciences. However, there is not a uniform way for validation of prior learning. Savonia University of Applied Sciences (SUAS) participates in an international project about Recognition of Prior Learning (RELATE) which develops RPL practices. The project aims to promote permeability into higher education programs within Germany, Estonia and Finland, and to create a model of agreement between vocational education and training (VET) and higher education (HE) institutions for simplified RPL. The project has fostered international co-operation and produced data about the good practices from different countries to clarify RPL guidelines. VET and HE teachers have gained practical knowledge of both educations and are now better able to continue competence-based-curriculum and RPL procedures development. It has also generated simulation as a new authentic method for RPL.

